    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d570">

                <pb facs="0789" n="775" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg775"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93/1765: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-17T17:00:00+07:00">April 17, 1944—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 17—3:17 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>669. In reply to Embassy’s inquiry, Consul at Tihwa, in telegram April 13,
                    locates Fuwen (also known as Keketohai) 300 li southwest Chenghwasze and 164 li
                    west of Chingho. Embassy’s 631, April 11.</p>
                <p>Consul preparing tracing of map showing disputed boundaries (Embassy’s 585, April
                    3) original of which obtained from Foreign Affairs Delegate.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
